Describe how the data will be obtained e:
By direct download from public data archives. No data are collected from participants, and no records at any covered entity are accessed.

This determination is intended to cover Open Spine Consortium projects that use datasets meeting ALL of the following criteria:
(1) the dataset is already publicly available and was de-identified by the source before public release;
(2) it can be downloaded by any member of the public without an application, request for permission, eligibility review, or data use agreement;
(3) it is not designated restricted-use by the source;
(4) the investigators receive no key, code, or other means of re-identification, and hold no relationship with the source enabling re-identification;
(5) no attempt is made to re-identify any individual, and no external data are linked to the dataset for the purpose of, or with the effect of, re-identification.

Examples of sources meeting these criteria include The Cancer Imaging Archive public collections, VerSe, and TotalSegmentator.

If a dataset does NOT meet all five criteria -- in particular if it is restricted-use, requires a proposal or application to obtain, or is governed by a data use agreement -- it falls outside this determination and a separate IRB submission will be made before that dataset is used.

Describe how the results will be usedapp:
Results are used to advance scientific and clinical understanding of spinal anatomy and its measurement, and are disseminated as:
- peer-reviewed journal articles and conference papers;
- openly licensed datasets of derived annotations, archived with a DOI;
- open-source software and trained models;
- educational and reference material for clinicians and researchers.

Findings are not applied to the care of any individual whose images appear in these datasets. No individual is contacted, and no result is returned to any individual, because no individual can be identified from the data.

Software released by the consortium is provided for research use only and is not offered for clinical diagnosis or treatment. Should the consortium later seek FDA clearance for any device, data supporting that application would fall under question 3.4 of this tool and a separate determination would be obtained beforehand.
